# `try: import X / except ImportError: / else:` -- the else clause was LOST when
# the try body was an import, and lost loudly: `error: expected expression` on
# the `else` line, for a decision the frontend had already made at the import.
#
# A guarded import is resolved at COMPILE time -- there is no runtime exception
# to catch, so the frontend picks the branch -- and that path had no `else` case
# at all. An unconsumed `else` then reached the caller as a statement and died
# there. The ordinary try path handled `else` correctly the whole time, which is
# why the failure looks like a general parser gap and is not one.
#
# TWO LAYERS RESOLVE IMPORTS AND BOTH HAD TO LEARN IT. The parser consumes the
# else; PyPreScanImports -- which walks every import token in the file with no
# notion of reachability -- now knows which ARM is dead and skips it. With only
# the parser taught, this file COMPILES and answers the dead arm's module.
#
# WHY THE SPELLING IS WORTH FIXING RATHER THAN WORKING AROUND. `else` is what
# keeps the selected arm's own import OUT of the try's exception coverage: with
# the second import inside the try, an ImportError raised by the module the try
# SELECTED is swallowed and silently redirected to the fallback. A seat
# rewriting lekkerzeilen's platform seam had to write the unfaithful spelling
# and leave a comment explaining why, which is the cost this removes.
# bug-n-try-except-else-does-not-parse-when-the-try-body-is-an-import
import nilpy_tryelse as t

# ARM 1 -- the handler ran, so the else must NOT have. Both spellings.
print("miss", t.miss())

# ARM 2 -- the else ran, so the handler must NOT have, and the else's own body,
# including a use of the module the try imported, must be live.
#
# THIS IS THE ROW A PARSER-ONLY FIX FAILS, and it is the reason the fixture has
# an arm whose guard RESOLVES at all. A unit alias is first-wins, so in the
# ordinary no-`else` idiom the live arm is lexically first in both outcomes and
# wins by position; an `else:` puts the live arm AFTER the dead handler and the
# dead handler's binding wins instead. Measured: `('else', 'fallback', ...)`
# against CPython's 'selected', exit 0, no diagnostic.
print("hit", t.hit())
